      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <!-- What to Gather Before You Start -->
  <h2>What to Gather Before You Start Filling Out the Template</h2>
  <p>Have these ready before you open any quitclaim deed template PDF or Word file:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>The existing deed</strong>: Your current deed is the source for the legal description, the
      current vesting (exact names as titleholders), and the APN. If you don't have a copy, the county
      recorder's office can provide one for a small fee, or you can request it through the county assessor's
      website.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Full legal names of all parties</strong>: Grantor(s) and grantee(s) exactly as they should
      appear on the new deed. For a person, this typically means first, middle, and last name. For an entity,
      the full registered name.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>How the grantee will hold title</strong>: If there are multiple grantees, you must decide on
      the vesting &mdash; joint tenancy, tenancy in common, or community property (in community property
      states). This affects inheritance rights and what happens when one owner dies.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>County and state</strong>: The recording requirements, transfer tax rates, and required form
      language all depend on which county the property sits in, not just the state.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Return address</strong>: Where the recorded deed gets mailed back.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <!-- How to Fill Out Each Field -->
  <h2>How to Fill Out Each Field Correctly</h2>

  <h3>Step 1: Recording Header</h3>
  <p>
    Leave the recorder-use-only boxes blank. Fill in the "return to" name and address. Add the APN if your
    county requires it (check the recorder's website or call ahead).
  </p>

  <h3>Step 2: Grantor</h3>
  <p>
    Type the full legal name(s) exactly matching the existing deed. If you see "John A. Smith and Mary B.
    Smith, husband and wife" on the current deed, that's how the grantor line must read.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 3: Grantee and Vesting</h3>
  <p>
    State the grantee's full name and the manner in which they'll hold title. Example: "John A. Smith and
    Mary B. Smith, as joint tenants with right of survivorship."
  </p>

  <h3>Step 4: Consideration</h3>
  <p>
    State the actual consideration or the legally appropriate consideration or exemption language required for
    the transaction and jurisdiction. Do not assume a nominal amount eliminates transfer tax &mdash; confirm
    the applicable treatment for your state and transaction type.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 5: Legal Description</h3>
  <p>
    Paste the full legal description verbatim from the existing deed. Do not abbreviate. If it runs onto a
    second page, that's fine &mdash; length is not a problem.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 6: Execution</h3>
  <p>
    Leave the signature lines blank until you are ready to execute. Follow the acknowledgment procedure
    required by your state. Do not pre-sign if the notary or applicable law requires the signature to be made
    in the notary's presence.
  </p>

  <!-- After You Download -->
  <h2>After You Download: Sign, Notarize, and Record</h2>
  <p>
    Getting the form is step one. After proper execution, record the deed promptly in the land records for
    the county where the property is located. Recording provides public notice and affects the deed's priority
    and effectiveness against third parties under applicable state law.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Follow your state's execution and acknowledgment requirements.</strong> Where acknowledgment
    before a notary is required, bring appropriate identification and complete the acknowledgment as required.
    Some states also require subscribing witnesses.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Record the deed.</strong> Take the original signed and notarized deed to the county recorder's
    or register of deeds' office in the county where the property is located. Recording offices may accept
    in-person, mail, or electronic submissions depending on the jurisdiction. Recording fees vary by
    jurisdiction and document length. Check the recorder's current fee schedule before filing.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Pay any applicable transfer tax.</strong> Even if consideration is nominal, some states charge a
    minimum transfer tax. Ask the recorder's office what's owed before you submit.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Retain a copy.</strong> After recording, obtain and retain the recorded instrument or
    certified or electronic copy according to the recorder's procedures. Keep it somewhere safe &mdash; it's
    your evidence the transfer was completed.
  </p>

  <!-- Ready to Fill Out -->
  <div class="cta-block">
    <h2 class="cta-block__heading">Ready to Fill Out Your Form?</h2>
    <p class="cta-block__body">
      The free quitclaim deed generator produces a printable quitclaim deed template tailored to your
